    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

That grant covers Clusterflick's own contribution — the screening listings and
the work of assembling them: which film is showing where and when, the venue
name, address and screen, the booking link, the performance notes, and the
normalisation and movie matching behind them.


How to attribute
----------------

CC BY 4.0 requires credit wherever the data is shown. Any of these will do:

    Markdown    Screening data from [Clusterflick](https://clusterflick.com) (CC BY 4.0)
    HTML        Screening data from <a href="https://clusterflick.com">Clusterflick</a> (CC BY 4.0)
    Plain text  Screening data from Clusterflick - clusterflick.com (CC BY 4.0)

Each feed carries the same credit in its X-WR-CALDESC property, so a feed that
has been subscribed to and separated from this repository still says where it
came from. Leave that property intact when you redistribute a feed.

The licence also asks that you indicate whether you changed the data.


What is not licensed
--------------------

Event descriptions embed film metadata from The Movie Database — the synopsis,
and the cast and director names where the venue did not supply its own. That
content belongs to TMDB and is used under the TMDB API terms of use.
Clusterflick cannot sublicense it, so it is excluded from the CC BY 4.0 grant
above. Your use of it is governed by TMDB's terms:

    https://www.themoviedb.org/api-terms-of-use

If you show any of it, read TMDB's attribution requirements and follow whatever
they ask for at the time — it is theirs to set, not ours to restate:

    https://www.themoviedb.org/api-terms-of-use#:~:text=3.%20Attribution

Because the TMDB material is woven into prose rather than held in separate
fields, a feed cannot be mechanically stripped back to only the CC BY 4.0
material. If you need that, build from the per-venue JSON in
clusterflick/data-transformed instead, where the TMDB fields are separable.


